# XenithOS Desktop, the system under the desktop: only on the ISO's list (an installed PC keeps
# what it has). The desktop, its apps and the security tools are in the other lists here, which
# become packages of their own so installed PCs get them with updates.
# The build leaves out Ubuntu's "recommended" extras, so each part that's needed is listed.
# The live system's own parts are added by live-build.

## The kernel: Ubuntu 24.04's newest (its "hardware enablement" one), for new PCs' parts
linux-image-generic-hwe-24.04

## The system
systemd-sysv
systemd-resolved
systemd-timesyncd
dbus
dbus-user-session
sudo
polkitd
pkexec
locales
console-setup
keyboard-configuration
tzdata
ucf
unattended-upgrades
apparmor
bash-completion
less
nano
ca-certificates
curl
wget
rsync
file
pciutils
usbutils
psmisc
xz-utils
zstd
unzip
zip
iproute2
iputils-ping
man-db
htop

## Hardware: firmware for graphics, Wi-Fi, Bluetooth and sound. Graphics use the open drivers
## (Intel, AMD, and nouveau on NVIDIA cards); NVIDIA's own driver isn't included.
linux-firmware
firmware-sof-signed
amd64-microcode
intel-microcode
network-manager
wpasupplicant
wireless-regdb
iw
bluez
upower
udisks2
exfatprogs
ntfs-3g
alsa-ucm-conf
alsa-utils
pipewire
pipewire-pulse
pipewire-alsa
wireplumber
libspa-0.2-bluetooth

## Starting up: the boot loaders the installer puts on the drive, and the start-up screen
## (Ubuntu's signed GRUB needs grub-efi-amd64, the one for UEFI PCs, or grub-pc, the one for
## BIOS PCs. They can't be installed together: the first is here and the installer swaps the
## second in on a BIOS PC. Nothing here may depend on grub-pc, or UEFI PCs get the wrong one.
## See hooks/0300-installer.hook.chroot.)
grub-common
grub2-common
grub-pc-bin
grub-efi-amd64-bin
grub-efi-amd64
grub-efi-amd64-signed
shim-signed
efibootmgr
plymouth

## Graphics
xserver-xorg
xinit
xauth
x11-xserver-utils
x11-xkb-utils
libgl1-mesa-dri
libglx-mesa0
libegl-mesa0
mesa-vulkan-drivers

## XenithOS Console. The Store builds its updates on the machine, so the compiler and the
## libraries' headers stay. The rest is what linux/install.sh adds for apps from Flathub.
build-essential
libsdl2-dev
zlib1g-dev
libx11-dev
libxtst6
freedoom
flatpak
cage
xwayland
xdg-desktop-portal
xdg-desktop-portal-gtk
